1
00:00:11,950 --> 00:00:13,150
 Hi Preppies!

2
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:15,550
I'm back with another exciting lesson.

3
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:18,790
Let's start today's lesson with this task.

4
00:00:19,370 --> 00:00:21,210
Alright, have you found the answer?

5
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:26,610
From the interactive question, you've just had an experience of handling

6
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:29,710
matching information to paragraphs question type.

7
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:33,150
Locate paragraphs containing given information.

8
00:00:33,910 --> 00:00:36,410
In today's lesson, we're going to learn about this.

9
00:00:37,090 --> 00:00:41,950
We will go back to the question at the beginning
after understanding steps for this question type.

10
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:45,010
This lesson has three parts.

11
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:51,930
1. Introduction to matching
information to paragraphs

12
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:57,030
2. Steps for matching information to paragraphs

13
00:00:57,790 --> 00:00:58,250
3. Practice.

14
00:00:59,370 --> 00:01:05,330
This lesson aims to help you understand
features of matching information to paragraphs.

15
00:01:05,990 --> 00:01:10,170
Understand and apply steps for matching information to paragraphs.

16
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:13,250
So what are we waiting for?

17
00:01:13,430 --> 00:01:16,270
Let's dive into the first part of today's lesson.

18
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:25,870
Matching information to paragraphs in IELTS reading is a question type which

19
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:30,050
requires candidates to match questions with paragraphs containing suitable

20
00:01:30,050 --> 00:01:31,890
information in the reading passage.

21
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:35,530
This question type has the following main parts.

22
00:01:37,090 --> 00:01:37,830
A requirement.

23
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:45,970
For those who take the paper-based test, please note
that the requirement is write the correct letter A to H.

24
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:51,250
So when filling in the answers, you only write the letters from A to H,

25
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:55,550
 not including the word section or paragraph A or paragraph H.

26
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:59,450
The remaining part is numbered questions.

27
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:04,850
Next, you need to pay attention to the types of information in the questions,

28
00:02:05,350 --> 00:02:05,930
including.

29
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:26,810
Definition.

30
00:02:28,910 --> 00:02:29,510
Explanation.

31
00:02:31,290 --> 00:02:31,890
Example.

32
00:02:34,190 --> 00:02:39,930
Next, let's take a look at some features of
this question type to help you tackle it better.

33
00:02:41,050 --> 00:02:44,990
In matching headings, in which you have to match headings with paragraphs,

34
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:47,150
your main task is to find the main idea.

35
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:52,230
In matching information to paragraphs, it requires you to match information

36
00:02:52,230 --> 00:02:56,190
with paragraphs or find a detailed piece of information in the paragraphs.

37
00:02:57,110 --> 00:03:02,590
The information for answering the questions may not be located in just one or

38
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:06,190
two sentences, but could be found in the whole paragraph.

39
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:12,090
Therefore, many times you have to find
very detailed information in the paragraph.

40
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:17,550
Moreover, you have to understand the overall meaning of the entire paragraph to

41
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:18,910
find the correct answer.

42
00:03:19,690 --> 00:03:24,490
The number of paragraphs is always more than the number of questions, so there

43
00:03:24,490 --> 00:03:27,390
will be options or paragraphs that are not used.

44
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:31,230
Check if the requirement includes this sentence.

45
00:03:32,110 --> 00:03:38,710
Note then, you may use any letters more than once, as in this example.

46
00:03:39,390 --> 00:03:44,430
It means that one paragraph can contain the information for two questions, and

47
00:03:44,430 --> 00:03:46,730
these two questions can have the same answer.

48
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:52,350
In the case where the requirement does not include the sentence, it means that

49
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:59,510
each answer can be written only once, and there
won't be any question having the same answer.

50
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:06,610
And the last feature is that the questions might
not come in order as the information in the passage.

51
00:04:08,010 --> 00:04:12,390
You should handle matching information to paragraphs questions after completing

52
00:04:12,390 --> 00:04:17,390
other question types because the information is located in the whole passage.

53
00:04:18,050 --> 00:04:22,050
After finishing questions that require finding detailed information, we can

54
00:04:22,050 --> 00:04:24,510
somewhat understand the content of the paragraphs.

55
00:04:24,910 --> 00:04:29,670
Hence, you can use scanning skill when
looking for detailed information more quickly.

56
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:34,050
So, we've gained the basic information about this question type.

57
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:36,070
How about the steps for it?

58
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:38,910
Let's find out about them in the next part.

59
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:46,520
There are three main steps for this question type.

60
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:52,280
Step 1. Read the requirement carefully
and underline keywords in questions.

61
00:04:53,140 --> 00:05:00,340
Step 2. Apply scanning skills to find keywords in
paragraphs corresponding to keywords in questions.

62
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:08,220
Step 3. Read paragraphs carefully, compare their
information with questions, and choose the answers.

63
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:13,000
Now, we're going to apply these steps to the following passage.

64
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:25,740
Let's take the first step together.

65
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:29,260
Step 1.
Read the requirement carefully.

66
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:32,800
We have five questions and four paragraphs.

67
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:35,400
A, B, C, D.

68
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:42,180
Because of the NB notes, don't assume that you will use every paragraph once.

69
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:47,200
Some might be used twice, or some might not be used at all.

70
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:53,940
Now that we've understood the requirements, let's
read and underline the keywords in question 1.

71
00:05:55,760 --> 00:06:01,540
Question 1. A reference to historical
methods used to promote home ownership.

72
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:07,980
The key phrase here is historical methods and promote home ownership.

73
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:13,160
We need to find a paragraph that talks about how things were done in the past

74
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:15,260
to help people buy homes.

75
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:24,740
Step 2. Apply scanning skills to find keywords in
paragraphs corresponding to keywords in questions.

76
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:29,640
Now, we scan the text for synonyms or related words.

77
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:34,200
In paragraph A, we immediately see the word historically.

78
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:36,780
This matches our keyword.

79
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:42,640
We also see promoted home ownership, which is an exact match.

80
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:49,940
And we see examples of methods like low
interest loans and suburban development.

81
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:54,280
Step 3.
Read paragraphs carefully.

82
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:58,460
Compare their information with questions and choose the answers.

83
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:01,880
Let's read paragraph A in detail.

84
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:07,880
In the decades following World War II, governments promoted home ownership

85
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:11,820
through low interest loans and suburban development.

86
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:18,860
Here, low interest loans and suburban development are the methods used.

87
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:25,580
Decades following World War II sets the historical context and the text

88
00:07:25,580 --> 00:07:29,540
explicitly says they promoted home ownership.

89
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:32,900
This perfectly matches question 1.

90
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:38,140
A reference to historical methods used to promote home ownership.

91
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:42,020
So, the answer to question 1 is A.

92
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:52,740
To practice this question type, we're going
to move on to the remaining questions.

93
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:57,900
Remember the note, you may use any letter more than once.

94
00:07:58,680 --> 00:08:05,100
So, paragraph A could be the answer again, or it could be B, C or D.

95
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:11,900
Preppies, please pause the video for four
minutes to finish questions 2 through 5.

96
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:16,940
Time's up.

97
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:18,800
Let's check question 2.

98
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:26,720
Question 2. Rent control may benefit tenants
initially, but can lead to neglected properties.

99
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:32,100
Scanning for rent control, we find it in paragraph C.

100
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:37,799
The text says rent control schemes may protect tenants in the short term.

101
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:41,320
This matches benefit tenants initially.

102
00:08:42,179 --> 00:08:47,980
It continues, but discouraged landlords
from maintaining or improving properties.

103
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:54,680
If a landlord stops maintaining a property, it becomes a neglected property.

104
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:58,620
So, paragraph C contains this information.

105
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:00,740
The answer is C.

106
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:08,400
Question 3. Examples of the social
consequences of high housing costs.

107
00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:15,500
Scanning for social and high housing costs, we go to paragraph D.

108
00:09:16,380 --> 00:09:22,580
The first sentence mentions the impact of
unaffordable housing on social well -being.

109
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:26,440
The text then lists specific examples.

110
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:29,100
1. Overcrowding.

111
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:32,360
2. Increased commuting times.

112
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:36,320
3. Displacement of lower income families.

113
00:09:37,140 --> 00:09:39,200
4. Social divide.

114
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:43,140
These are all examples of social consequences.

115
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:46,180
Therefore, the answer is D.

116
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:54,580
Question 4. An explanation of why housing
supply fails to meet demand in cities.

117
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:00,460
The key words supply and demand leads us to paragraph B.

118
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:06,640
The text explicitly mentions the imbalance between supply and demand.

119
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:10,080
It explains why supply fails.

120
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:16,640
Planning restrictions, limited land
availability and lengthy approval processes.

121
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:21,560
These are the reasons, the explanation for the failure.

122
00:10:22,220 --> 00:10:24,180
So, the answer is B.

123
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:30,680
Question 5. A statement on how
difficult purchasing a home has become.

124
00:10:31,220 --> 00:10:34,160
We need to find a statement about difficulty.

125
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:37,320
Let's look at paragraph A again.

126
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:43,420
The last sentence says, what was once a relatively straightforward path to

127
00:10:43,420 --> 00:10:48,140
ownership has become, for many, an almost impossible dream.

128
00:10:49,160 --> 00:10:54,880
Describing home ownership as an almost impossible dream is a very strong

129
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:57,820
statement about how difficult it has become.

130
00:10:58,540 --> 00:11:00,680
So, the answer is A.

131
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:07,160
Notice that we used paragraph A for both question 1 and question 5.

132
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:15,900
This confirms why the note NB, you may use
any letter more than once, was so important.

133
00:11:17,420 --> 00:11:21,480
We've dealt with an example of matching information to paragraphs.

134
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:25,200
Let's recap the key points of this lesson together.

135
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:34,600
You should pay attention to the important features of matching information to

136
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:37,220
paragraphs mentioned at the beginning of the lesson.

137
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:43,600
Task. Matching information with paragraphs
to find detailed information in the passage.

138
00:11:44,420 --> 00:11:49,360
Find detailed information and understand the overall meaning of the paragraph

139
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:50,720
to find the answer.

140
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:58,320
The number of paragraphs is more than the
questions, so some paragraphs are not used.

141
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:01,640
Check if the requirement includes this sentence.

142
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:06,340
Note NB, you may use any letters more than once.

143
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:11,220
Questions might not come in order as the information in the passage.

144
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:16,260
Moreover, you need to remember three steps for this question type.

145
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:21,760
Step 1. Read the requirement carefully
and underline keywords in questions.

146
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:29,340
Step 2. Apply scanning skills to find keywords in
paragraphs corresponding to keywords in questions.

147
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:36,820
Step 3. Read paragraphs carefully, compare the
information with questions, and choose the answers.

148
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:39,860
For now, goodbye and see you in the next lesson.

